content.text:
Now check the racing result summary bug when forced finish isn't relevant. But there might be a real bug in standings with finished players: AI finished → score = laps+1, player finished → laps+2, so player always ahead of any AI that finished. That's fine for player's own result? For a real race, AI may finish earlier; their finish times should decide. But since player's finish triggers results screen, all AI that finished before should rank ahead. Current scoring gives player laps+2, ahead of AI laps+1, so player is always 1st if they finish, even if AI finished earlier. Actually currentRank uses same formula: playerScore = passed/N = laps if just finished; AI score laps+1 if finished, so player would be last among finishers. But finishRace sets this.rank = currentRank(true) BEFORE updating player.passed? In passGate, this.player.passed = laps*N then finishRace() is called; currentRank playerScore = passed/N = laps; AI finished score = laps+1 → rank behind all finished AI. So rank is correct at finish. But buildSummary uses standings() where player score = laps+2, which then sorts the player above all AI. Also completeRace reward uses summary.pos from buildSummary! Let me check: buildSummary returns pos = playerRow.pos from standings. So the results modal and rewards use the inflated standings, always showing 1st for the player who finishes, regardless of actual rank. That is a real bug!

Let's examine: standings() sets player score = this.finished ? this.laps + 2 : passed/N; AI finished score = this.laps + 1. So player is always > AI. This makes results show 1st even if AI finished earlier. In our forced test it showed 1st. In a real race with fast AI, the player would still be 1st on results. That undermines the whole race. Need to fix standings to use finish times / progress correctly.

Better implementation: build a unified score:
- For each participant: if finished, score = laps + 1 - (finishTime / bigNumber) so earlier finishes rank higher; or sort by finished status and time.
- If not finished, score = (lap-1+u) progress.
We need player progress too.

Simplest: use currentRank's logic and sort with a comparator:
- For finished racers, rank by finishTime ascending (smaller better).
- Unfinished by progress descending.
- Finished always ahead of unfinished.

Build players array with {player, finished, time, progress}. Sort:
 if a.finished && b.finished → a.time - b.time
 if a.finished !== b.finished → a.finished ? -1 : 1
 else → b.progress - a.progress

For player:
- finished = this.finished, time = this.finishTime, progress = this.player.passed / this.N.
For AI:
- finished = ai.finished, time=ai.finishTime, progress = ai.lap - 1 + ai.u.

Then assign pos. That fixes result standings and rank.

Also currentRank() uses score comparisons; it's okay except AI `ai.u` meaning? But can use the same ranking.

Let's rewrite standings() and currentRank() using a helper `_rankEntries()`.

Implement:

rankEntries() {
  const list = [{
    name: profile.name, player: true,
    finished: this.finished,
    time: this.finished ? this.finishTime : null,
    progress: this.player.passed / this.N,
  }];
  for (const ai of this.ais) {
    list.push({
      name: ai.name, player: false,
      finished: ai.finished,
      time: ai.finished ? ai.finishTime : null,
      progress: ai.lap - 1 + ai.u,
    });
  }
  list.sort((a, b) => {
    if (a.finished && b.finished) return a.time - b.time;
    if (a.finished !== b.finished) return a.finished ? -1 : 1;
    return b.progress - a.progress;
  });
  list.forEach((r, i) => { r.pos = i + 1; });
  return list;
}

standings() {
  return this.rankEntries().map(r => ({ name, player, score: r.progress, time: r.time, pos: r.pos }));
}

currentRank(force) {
  if (this.finished && !force) return this.rank;
  return this.rankEntries().find(r => r.player)?.pos ?? 1;
}

Wait, for finished racers, progress may be laps; score field used only for display? buildSummary spreads standings; result table uses r.timeText and gap and pos. Fine.

Gap calculation: `gap: r.time && this.finishTime ? r.time - this.finishTime : null` — for AI finished after player, positive. For AI finished before player, negative. UI may display absolute? It may show "+" wrong. Not critical.

Need to check ai.u meaning. In updateAIs, ai.u likely normalized segment progress 0..1. Search.

